      id: "why-efficiency",
      title: "Why efficiency still matters",
      pages: "Slides 3–11",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "The deck opens with a compact definition that frames the whole course: a solution to a problem needs **a way to organize data in memory** — a data structure — and **a sequence of steps** that solves the problem — an algorithm." },
        { type: "formula", text: "Program = Data Structure + Algorithm" },
        { type: "p", text: "A primary concern of the course is **efficiency**: efficient data structures and efficient algorithms. It is tempting to assume that faster hardware removes the need to study efficiency. The slides reject that assumption: more powerful computers make more complex applications possible, and those applications demand more computation. Without efficient design, some problems remain computationally infeasible — taking years, or millions of years." },
        { type: "callout", tone: "source", title: "From the slides (p. 9)", text: "Every data structure has costs in **space** per item stored (data plus overhead), **time** per basic operation, and **programming effort**. A more complicated structure is not automatically a better one." },
        { type: "h3", text: "The bank-account example" },
        { type: "p", text: "Different operations have different frequency and urgency. For a bank account, opening an account should take a few minutes, transactions a few seconds, and closing an account can be handled overnight. A structure should therefore be chosen from the **operation mix**, not from a vague sense that “faster is better”." },
        { type: "table", caption: "Operation costs shape the choice (source p. 10)", head: ["Operation", "Expected cost", "Design implication"], rows: [
          ["Open account", "A few minutes", "May afford extra setup work"],
          ["Transaction", "A few seconds", "Search and update must be efficient"],
          ["Close / delete", "Can run overnight", "Deletion may be comparatively expensive"]
        ] },
        { type: "callout", tone: "tip", title: "Rule of thumb", text: "Pick a data structure that is cheap for the operations you perform most often, and accept higher cost for rare operations." },
        { type: "h3", text: "Questions to ask before choosing a data structure (p. 8)" },
        { type: "p", text: "Before choosing between candidate structures, the slides suggest asking a few design questions about the problem itself." },
        { type: "ul", items: [
          "Are all the data inserted into the data structure at the beginning, or are insertions interspersed with other operations?",
          "Can data be deleted? If so, a more complex representation is typically required.",
          "Are all the data processed in some well-defined order, or is random access allowed?"
        ] },
        { type: "callout", tone: "source", title: "Data structure philosophy (p. 10)", text: "We should choose a data structure that has little concern for the cost of deletion, but is highly efficient for search and moderately efficient for insertion." },
        { type: "h3", text: "The goals of the course (p. 11)" },
        { type: "ol", items: [
