    {
      id: "adt",
      title: "Data types, data items, and abstract data types",
      pages: "Slides 12–15, 19–20",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "The slides build the vocabulary in layers, starting from a *type*: a collection of values such as `Boolean = {true, false}` or the integers. A **data item** is a piece of information whose value is drawn from a type. An item is a *member* of that type." },
        { type: "ul", items: [
          "**Simple data item** — contains no subparts, e.g. one integer.",
          "**Aggregate data item** — contains several pieces of information, e.g. a bank account with name, address, account number, and balance."
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "A **data type** is a type together with a collection of operations that manipulate values of that type (for example, addition on integers)." },
        { type: "callout", tone: "source", title: "ADT definition (p. 13)", text: "An **Abstract Data Type (ADT)** is a definition of a data type solely in terms of a set of values and a set of operations on those values. An ADT specifies **what** can be stored (attributes) and **what operations** can be performed — not how the operations are implemented." },
        { type: "p", text: "The employee example makes the split concrete. The ADT stores employees with their relevant attributes and supports hiring, firing, and retiring. **Encapsulation** hides the implementation details behind that contract." },
        { type: "p", text: "A **data structure** is the physical implementation of an ADT. Each ADT operation is implemented by one or more subroutines (member functions or methods), and the variables that define the required space are the data members (attributes). A *file structure* is the corresponding organization of data on peripheral storage." },
        { type: "table", caption: "ADT versus data structure (source p. 15)", head: ["", "ADT", "Data structure"], rows: [
          ["Viewpoint", "User’s point of view", "Programmer’s point of view"],
          ["Role", "Model of a data type: data + operations", "Concrete organization of the encapsulated data"],
          ["Contents", "Definition only", "Data members + functions"],
          ["Number of versions", "One definition", "Many possible implementations"],
          ["Key property", "Separates definition from implementation; implementation-independent", "Concrete implementation"],
          ["Analogy", "Like a Java interface", "A class that implements the interface"]
        ] },
        { type: "h3", text: "Logical and physical forms" },
        { type: "p", text: "Every data type has both forms. The **logical form** is its definition as an ADT; the **physical form** is its implementation as a data structure. When you implement an ADT you work with the physical form. When you use an ADT elsewhere in a program, you should need to think only about the logical form." },
        { type: "p", text: "This is abstraction in action. The slides compare it to hierarchies such as transistors → gates → CPU: each level hides the level below. In a program, implement the ADT once, then think only about the ADT, not its implementation." },
        { type: "callout", tone: "info", title: "Why this matters for the rest of the course", text: "Chapters 2 and 3 implement the *same* List ADT in different ways — with arrays and with linked nodes. The interface stays stable; the cost profile changes." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "list-adt",
      title: "The List ADT: one contract, several implementations",
      pages: "Slides 16–18",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "A **list** is a finite, ordered sequence of data items called elements, each having a position. The notation is `<a₀, a₁, …, aₙ₋₁>`. “Ordered” refers to the **positions**, not to the values: an unsorted list is still an ordered sequence because element 0 comes before element 1." },
        { type: "p", text: "The empty list contains no elements. The **length** is the number of elements currently stored. The beginning is the **head**; the end is the **tail**." },
        { type: "p", text: "For a list of integers, the slides specify the following operations without committing to an implementation:" },
        { type: "ul", items: [
          "Insert a new integer at a particular position.",
          "Return `true` if the list is empty.",
          "Reinitialize the list.",
          "Return the number of integers currently in the list.",
          "Delete the integer at a particular position."
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "There are two standard ways to implement this ADT: an **array-based list** using contiguous memory, or a **linked list** using dynamic allocation. The List is the ADT; a linked list is a pointer-based *representation* of a List." },
        { type: "diagram", title: "One contract, two physical forms", text: String.raw`            List ADT
   (values + operations, no layout)
          /            \
         /              \
 Array-based list     Linked list
 contiguous cells     nodes + links
 index arithmetic     pointer chasing` },
        { type: "callout", tone: "source", title: "Why more than one implementation? (p. 18)", text: "Different applications use the operations with different frequencies and running-time requirements. That is exactly why an ADT can have more than one implementation." },
        { type: "p", text: "A first sketch of the contract in C++ makes the separation visible. The abstract class below is a *fragment*: it declares operations and stores no layout. Chapter 3 turns it into a full pure-virtual interface." },
        { type: "code", title: "A minimal List contract (fragment — not a complete program)", language: "cpp", code: String.raw`// The ADT says WHAT is supported, not HOW it is stored.
class List {
public:
    virtual ~List() = default;
    virtual void clear() = 0;                 // remove all elements
    virtual bool insert(int item) = 0;        // insert at current position
    virtual bool append(int item) = 0;        // insert at the tail
    virtual bool remove() = 0;                // remove current element
    virtual int  length() const = 0;          // number of elements
    virtual bool setPos(int pos) = 0;         // move current position
    virtual bool getValue(int& out) const = 0; // read current element
    virtual void print() const = 0;
};` },
        { type: "p", text: "Nothing in that declaration says “array” or “pointer”. That is the point of the ADT." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "classification",
      title: "Classifying data structures",
      pages: "Slides 22–26",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "A data structure is a way of storing data in a computer so that it can be used efficiently. It specifies the elements, the relationships between them, and the operations that may be performed on them. The study of data structures is the study of the **storage, retrieval, and manipulation** of information." },
        { type: "p", text: "The library example shows why arrangement matters: books can be placed on a shelf arbitrarily or organized by title, author, and so on. The same collection supports different operations at different costs depending on the arrangement." },
        { type: "h3", text: "Operations on data structures" },
        { type: "p", text: "The deck groups the fundamental operations into four verbs: **create**, **destroy**, **access**, and **update**." },
        { type: "h3", text: "Primitive versus non-primitive" },
        { type: "table", caption: "Two broad classes (source pp. 24–25)", head: ["Class", "Definition", "Examples"], rows: [
          ["Primitive", "Basic structures operated on directly by machine instructions", "Integers, floating-point numbers, characters, string constants, pointers"],
          ["Non-primitive", "More sophisticated structures that group homogeneous or heterogeneous items and are built from primitives", "Arrays, lists, files, linked lists, trees, graphs"]
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "Non-primitive structures are further divided by **memory layout**:" },
        { type: "ul", items: [
          "**Linear** — allocation is sequential/contiguous: arrays, stacks, queues, linked lists.",
          "**Non-linear** — allocation is not sequential, but random or discontinuous: trees, graphs."
        ] },
        { type: "callout", tone: "warn", title: "A subtlety", text: "The slides classify linked lists as linear because the *logical* order is one-dimensional, even though the nodes are not contiguous in memory. Linearity is about the relationship between elements, not about addresses." },
        { type: "diagram", title: "Classification used in the slides", text: String.raw`Data structures
├── Primitive ........ int, float, char, pointer, string constant
└── Non-primitive
    ├── Linear ....... array, stack, queue, linked list
    └── Non-linear ... tree, graph` }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "algorithms",
      title: "Algorithms and the properties they must satisfy",
      pages: "Slides 27–29",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "An **algorithm** is a clearly specified set of simple instructions to be followed to solve a problem. It takes a set of values as input and produces a value, or set of values, as output. It may be written in English, as pseudo-code, or as a computer program." },
        { type: "p", text: "A problem can have many algorithms. A **computer program** is one concrete representation of an algorithm in a particular programming language. One algorithm may be better than another for a specific variation of the problem or a specific class of inputs — one sort may win on small integer collections, another on large ones, a third on variable-length strings." },
        { type: "p", text: "The slides list eight properties. They are useful as a checklist when you design or review an algorithm." },
        { type: "table", caption: "Algorithm properties (source p. 29)", head: ["#", "Property", "What it means"], rows: [
          ["1", "Correctness", "It computes the proper function."],
          ["2", "Concrete steps", "Each step is completely understood and doable by the machine."],
          ["3", "Determinism", "There is no ambiguity about which step is performed next."],
          ["4", "Finiteness", "It is composed of a finite number of steps."],
          ["5", "Termination", "It must not enter an infinite loop."],
          ["6", "Efficiency", "It solves the problem with the least computational resources (time and space) reasonably available."],
          ["7", "Generality", "It is valid on all possible inputs, not just one example."],
          ["8", "Input/Output", "There is a specified number of input values and one or more result values."]
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "A useful exercise is to write two correct algorithms for the same problem and compare them. The program below computes the sum 1 + 2 + … + n twice: once with a loop and once with the closed-form formula n(n+1)/2. Both are correct; the second does constant work." },
        { type: "code", title: "Two algorithms, one problem", language: "cpp", verified: "MSVC 19.44 · /std:c++17 /EHsc", code: String.raw`#include <iostream>

// Algorithm A: explicit accumulation, O(n) additions.
long long sum_by_loop(int n) {
    long long total = 0;
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
        total += i;
    }
    return total;
}

// Algorithm B: closed form, O(1) arithmetic operations.
long long sum_by_formula(int n) {
    return static_cast<long long>(n) * (n + 1) / 2;
}

int main() {
    for (int n : {10, 100, 1000}) {
        std::cout << "n=" << n
                  << " loop=" << sum_by_loop(n)
                  << " formula=" << sum_by_formula(n) << '\n';
    }
}` },
        { type: "callout", tone: "info", title: "What the example shows", text: "Correctness and efficiency are separate questions. The loop is easy to verify by inspection; the formula is faster but needs an argument that it computes the same function. Asymptotic analysis is how we compare them without timing every machine." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "counting-operations",
      title: "Measuring cost by counting operations",
      pages: "Slides 30–47",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "**Algorithm analysis** is the process of determining how much computing time and storage an algorithm will require — predicting resource requirements in a given environment. Programmers have two goals that sometimes conflict: write code that is easy to understand, and make efficient use of the machine. The first belongs to software engineering; the second is the concern of data structures and algorithm analysis." },
        { type: "h3", text: "Empirical versus asymptotic" },
        { type: "table", caption: "Two ways to measure (source p. 31)", head: ["Approach", "How it works", "Weakness"], rows: [
          ["Computational / empirical", "Run programs and compare timings", "Hard to do fairly; depends on programming effort, test-case choice, machine load, OS, and compiler"],
          ["Asymptotic analysis", "Predict growth from the code’s structure", "Ignores constant factors and implementation detail by design"]
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "**Time complexity** estimates the number of operations required to solve a problem of size n. **Space complexity** estimates the memory required. Running time is written `T(n)` for a function T on input size n." },
        { type: "h3", text: "The counting rules" },
        { type: "ol", items: [
          "Assume an arbitrary time unit.",
          "Count 1 unit for an assignment, a single input/output operation, a single Boolean operation, a single arithmetic operation, or a function return.",
          "For a selection statement (`if`, `switch`), add the cost of evaluating the condition to the **maximum** of the costs of the individual clauses.",
          "For a loop, multiply the cost of the body by the number of iterations. For nested loops, analyze inside-out and multiply the sizes of all loops. Assume the loop executes the maximum number of iterations possible.",
          "For a function call, count 1 for setup, plus any parameter calculations, plus the cost of executing the body."
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "These rules produce an **exact count** for a fixed convention. The slides emphasize that there is no universally accepted rule set; an exact count is simply a common, useful convention." },
        { type: "h3", text: "Worked counts from the slides" },
        { type: "table", caption: "Exact counts (source pp. 36–40)", head: ["Fragment", "Counting argument", "T(n)"], rows: [
          ["`count()` — one loop with two operations in the body", "1 assignment + 1 output + 1 input + (1 + (n+1) tests + n increments) + 2n + 1 return", "**4n + 6**"],
          ["`func()` — two while loops", "Five leading units + (n+1 tests + 2n) + (n tests + n−1 increments)", "**5n + 5**"],
          ["Two nested n×n loops", "1 + 2(1 + (n+1) + n) + 2n²", "**2n² + 4n + 5**"],
          ["`if/else` where the `then` branch loops n times", "1 + 1 + Max(4n + 2, 1)", "**4n + 4**"]
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "The fourth row illustrates the selection rule: the `else` branch does constant work, so the maximum is the cost of the `then` branch." },
        { type: "h3", text: "Formal approach" },
        { type: "p", text: "For asymptotic work, we simplify by ignoring initializations, loop-control conditions, and counter increments. The primary consideration becomes the number of **basic operations** required to process an input of a certain size. A basic operation must take time independent of the particular operand values — adding or comparing two integers qualifies; summing an array of n integers does not, because its cost depends on n." },
        { type: "ul", items: [
          "**Size** is the number of inputs processed; for sorting, it is the number of records.",
          "A `for` loop translates to a summation whose index and bounds match the loop.",
          "Consecutive statements add their running times.",
          "A conditional statement takes the maximum of its branches."
        ] },
        { type: "code", title: "Instrumenting a loop (compile-and-run check)", language: "cpp", verified: "MSVC 19.44 · /std:c++17 /EHsc", code: String.raw`#include <iostream>

// Count how many times the body of this specific loop runs.
// This is instrumentation, not a general timing tool.
long long count_body_executions(int n) {
    long long executions = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        ++executions;
    }
    return executions;
}

int main() {
    for (int n : {0, 1, 5, 42}) {
        std::cout << "n=" << n
                  << " body executions=" << count_body_executions(n) << '\n';
    }
}` },
        { type: "callout", tone: "warn", title: "Do not confuse a count with a time", text: "A count of operations is a model. Two machines can execute the same count at different speeds, and a compiler can change the constants. Asymptotic analysis deliberately keeps only the growth." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "growth-rates",
      title: "Growth rates and asymptotic intuition",
      pages: "Slides 48–52",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Asymptotic analysis estimates the time or space required as a function of input size. It analyzes the time required for an algorithm and the space required for a data structure. The **growth rate** is the rate at which cost grows as input size grows." },
        { type: "table", caption: "Growth-rate families named in the slides (p. 51)", head: ["T(n)", "Name"], rows: [
          ["`c`", "constant"],
          ["`log n`", "logarithmic"],
          ["`n`", "linear"],
          ["`n log n`", "“n log n”"],
          ["`n²`", "quadratic"],
          ["`n³`", "cubic"],
          ["`2ⁿ`", "exponential"]
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "The ordering matters more than the exact values. The table below uses computed values to make the separation visible; it is an illustration, not a claim from the slides." },
        { type: "table", caption: "Computed comparison (base-2 logarithm; powers shown approximately)", head: ["n", "log₂ n", "n log₂ n", "n²", "n³", "2ⁿ"], rows: [
          ["10", "≈ 3.3", "≈ 33", "100", "1,000", "1,024"],
          ["100", "≈ 6.6", "≈ 664", "10,000", "1,000,000", "≈ 1.27 × 10³⁰"],
          ["1,000", "≈ 10", "≈ 9,966", "1,000,000", "10⁹", "≈ 1.07 × 10³⁰¹"]
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "For small n, a quadratic algorithm can beat a logarithmic one because the constants are smaller. As n grows, the higher-order term dominates and the gap becomes decisive. This is why the course studies the shape of T(n), not just one measurement." },
        { type: "callout", tone: "tip", title: "Reading the table", text: "Doubling n doubles a linear cost, roughly doubles-plus a little for n log n, quadruples a quadratic cost, and *squares* an exponential cost. Exponential growth is the reason some problems are called computationally infeasible." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "cases",
      title: "Best, worst, and average case",
      pages: "Slides 53, 59, 64",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Some algorithms take different amounts of time on different inputs of the same size. The canonical example is **sequential search** for a key K in an array of n integers: start at the first element and inspect each element in turn until K is found." },
        { type: "table", caption: "Sequential search on n elements (source p. 53)", head: ["Case", "Input that produces it", "Comparisons", "Bound"], rows: [
          ["Best", "K is in the first position", "1", "O(1)"],
          ["Worst", "K is in the last position, or absent", "n", "O(n)"],
          ["Average", "K is equally likely to be in any position", "(n + 1) / 2", "O(n)"]
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "The average comes from summing the positions 1 + 2 + … + n = n(n+1)/2 and dividing by n. Some presentations include the absent case; the slides use the equally-likely-among-the-n-positions assumption and state `(n+1)/2` explicitly." },
        { type: "callout", tone: "warn", title: "Common misunderstanding (p. 59)", text: "“The best case is n = 1 because that is the fastest” is **wrong**. Big-Oh describes growth as n grows. The best case is the *cheapest input of size n among all inputs of size n*, not the smallest n." },
        { type: "p", text: "A related misunderstanding is to confuse **worst case** with **upper bound**. An upper bound is a statement about a growth rate. The worst case is a particular input class: the most expensive input among all inputs of a given size. The two ideas meet when we say “the algorithm is in O(f(n)) in the worst case”." },
        { type: "callout", tone: "source", title: "A contrasting example (p. 53)", text: "A “largest-value sequential search” that must examine every array value always does n comparisons, so its best, average, and worst cases are all Θ(n). Having three case names does not guarantee three different costs." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "notation",
      title: "Big-Oh, Big-Omega, Big-Theta, and the simplification rules",
      pages: "Slides 54–70",
      blocks: [
        { type: "h3", text: "Upper bounds: Big-Oh" },
        { type: "p", text: "An **upper bound** indicates the highest growth rate an algorithm can have. Big-Oh notation formalizes it for a chosen class of inputs (best, average, or worst)." },
        { type: "callout", tone: "source", title: "Definition (p. 55)", text: "For T(n) a non-negatively valued function, T(n) is in the set **O(f(n))** if there exist two positive constants **c** and **n₀** such that **T(n) ≤ c·f(n)** for all **n > n₀**." },
        { type: "p", text: "`n₀` is the smallest value of n for which the claim of an upper bound holds. Meaning: for all data sets big enough, the algorithm always executes in fewer than `c·f(n)` steps in the specified case. Big-Oh says “grows no faster than”; it is a set membership claim, not a strict equality." },
        { type: "ul", items: [
          "If `T(n) = 3n²`, then T(n) is in O(n²). It is also in O(n³), but we want the **tightest** upper bound, so we prefer O(n²).",
          "Average sequential search: `T(n) = c_s·n/2`. For n > 1, `c_s·n/2 ≤ c_s·n`, so T(n) is in O(n) with `n₀ = 1` and `c = c_s` (p. 57).",
          "If `T(n) = c₁n² + c₂n` in the average case, then for n > 1, `c₁n² + c₂n ≤ c₁n² + c₂n² = (c₁ + c₂)n²`, so T(n) is in O(n²) with `c = c₁ + c₂` and `n₀ = 1` (p. 58).",
          "If `T(n) = c`, we say it is in O(1) (p. 58)."
        ] },
        { type: "h3", text: "Lower bounds: Big-Omega" },
        { type: "callout", tone: "source", title: "Definition (p. 61)", text: "T(n) is in the set **Ω(g(n))** if there exist two positive constants c and n₀ such that **T(n) ≥ c·g(n)** for all **n > n₀**." },
        { type: "p", text: "Big-Omega describes the least amount of a resource the algorithm needs for a class of inputs. We want the **greatest** (tightest) lower bound possible. Example: for `T(n) = c₁n² + c₂n`, we have `T(n) ≥ c₁n²` for all n > 1, so T(n) is in Ω(n²) with `c = c₁` and `n₀ = 1`." },
        { type: "h3", text: "When both bounds meet: Big-Theta" },
        { type: "callout", tone: "source", title: "Definition (p. 63)", text: "An algorithm is **Θ(h(n))** if it is in O(h(n)) **and** it is in Ω(h(n))." },
        { type: "p", text: "For polynomial equations on T(n), we always have Θ: once the equation is known, the analysis is “complete” in the sense that the growth rate is pinned down. For example, `3n² + 5n + 2` is Θ(n²)." },
        { type: "table", caption: "Notation summary", head: ["Notation", "Inequality", "Mental model"], rows: [
          ["O(f(n))", "T(n) ≤ c·f(n) for n > n₀", "Grows no faster than f"],
          ["Ω(g(n))", "T(n) ≥ c·g(n) for n > n₀", "Grows at least as fast as g"],
          ["Θ(h(n))", "Both of the above", "Grows like h, up to constants"]
        ] },
        { type: "h3", text: "Four simplification rules (p. 65)" },
        { type: "ol", items: [
          "If f(n) ∈ O(g(n)) and g(n) ∈ O(h(n)), then f(n) ∈ O(h(n)). (Transitivity.)",
          "If f(n) ∈ O(k·g(n)) for any constant k > 0, then f(n) ∈ O(g(n)). (Ignore constants.)",
          "If f₁(n) ∈ O(g₁(n)) and f₂(n) ∈ O(g₂(n)), then (f₁ + f₂)(n) ∈ O(max(g₁(n), g₂(n))). (Keep the more expensive part; drop lower-order terms.)",
          "If f₁(n) ∈ O(g₁(n)) and f₂(n) ∈ O(g₂(n)), then f₁(n)·f₂(n) ∈ O(g₁(n)·g₂(n)). (Repeated actions multiply.)"
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "Applying the first three rules means ignoring constants and lower-order terms. Example: if `T(n) = 3n⁴ + 5n²`, then T(n) is in O(n⁴); the n² term contributes relatively little as n becomes large." },
        { type: "h3", text: "Running-time examples" },
        { type: "table", caption: "Source pp. 67–70", head: ["Code shape", "Reasoning", "Result"], rows: [
          ["`a = b;`", "Constant-time assignment", "Θ(1)"],
          ["`sum = 0;` then a loop of n iterations doing `sum += n;`", "1 + n constant-time iterations; keep the larger", "Θ(n)"],
          ["Double loop `for i=1..n` / `for j=1..i` plus a separate O(n) loop", "Triangular inner work is Θ(n²); adding a linear loop does not change the maximum", "Θ(n²)"],
          ["Double loop n×n versus triangular loop", "n² versus (n+1)n/2 — both quadratic", "Both Θ(n²)"]
        ] },
        { type: "callout", tone: "info", title: "Other control statements (p. 70)", text: "A `while` loop is analyzed like a `for` loop. An `if` takes the greater complexity of the two clauses (assuming the branch probabilities do not depend on n). A `switch` takes the most expensive case. A function call adds the cost of the subroutine." },
        { type: "p", text: "One caution from the slides: the running-time examples contain typographical errors in the loop headers (for example, incrementing the wrong variable). The *analysis* is the intended lesson; the code in this academy has been cleaned up to valid C++." }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: "space",
      title: "Space bounds and the space/time trade-off",
      pages: "Slides 71–74",
      blocks: [
        { type: "p", text: "Space bounds can be analyzed with the same asymptotic tools. Time requirements are normally measured for an algorithm that manipulates a data structure; space requirements are normally determined for the data structure itself." },
        { type: "p", text: "A data structure’s primary purpose is to store data in a way that allows efficient access. To provide that access it stores additional information where the data are — **overhead** (links, metadata, unused capacity, alignment)." },
        { type: "table", caption: "Space examples (source pp. 72–73)", head: ["Structure", "Reasoning", "Space"], rows: [
          ["Array of n integers", "Each integer takes c bytes, so the array takes cn bytes", "Θ(n)"],
          ["Friendship matrix for n people", "An n×n array; row i marks person i’s friends, and the relation is symmetric", "Θ(n²)"]
        ] },
        { type: "p", text: "The friendship example is worth reading carefully: for n people, a full n×n table stores n² entries even though only the pairs matter. A different representation (for example, adjacency lists) can reduce space at the cost of different access times. That is a trade-off, not a free win." },
        { type: "h3", text: "The space/time trade-off principle" },
        { type: "p", text: "One can often reduce time by sacrificing space, or reduce space by sacrificing time. The slides give three illustrations:" },
        { type: "ul", items: [
          "**Encoding or packing** information reduces storage, but unpacking or decoding requires additional time.",
          "A **lookup table** for factorials can replace repeated computation. For a program that often computes factorials, pre-computing the storable values (up to 12! for a 32-bit int) is likely much more time-efficient.",
          "**Disk-based trade-off:** the smaller you make the disk storage requirements, the faster the program can run, because the time to read information from disk is enormous compared with computation time."
        ] },
        { type: "callout", tone: "warn", title: "Read the disk claim as an I/O argument", text: "The slide’s wording is deliberately strong. The underlying point is that fewer bytes and fewer reads mean less I/O latency; it is not a claim that compression is always free. Decoding still costs CPU time." },
        { type: "p", text: "The practical lesson: state which resource you are optimizing. A structure that is excellent for time may be unacceptable for memory, and vice versa." }
      ]
    }
  ],
