  exercises: [
    {
      id: "ch1-ex-1",
      title: "Count a rectangular double loop",
      prompt: "Using the chapter’s exact-count convention, derive T(n, m) for:\n\n```cpp\nint sum = 0;\nfor (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i)\n    for (int j = 1; j <= m; ++j)\n        sum++;\n```\nCount the initialization, each loop’s assignment/test/increment, and 2 units per execution of `sum++`. Then give the asymptotic growth in terms of n and m.",
      hint: "The inner loop runs n times. Each execution of the inner loop costs (2m + 2) for its own control, and the body costs 2 per (i, j) pair.",
      solution: "Initialization: 1.\nOuter loop control: 1 assignment + (n+1) tests + n increments = 2n + 2.\nInner loop control: executed n times; each execution costs 1 assignment + (m+1) tests + m increments = 2m + 2, so n(2m + 2).\nBody: 2 units for each of the nm pairs = 2nm.\n\nT(n, m) = 1 + (2n + 2) + n(2m + 2) + 2nm = 4nm + 4n + 3.\n\nIgnoring constants and lower-order terms gives Θ(nm). If n = m, this is Θ(n²)."
    },
    {
      id: "ch1-ex-2",
      title: "Count a triangular double loop",
      prompt: "Derive T(n) for:\n\n```cpp\nint sum = 0;\nfor (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i)\n    for (int j = 1; j <= i; ++j)\n        sum++;\n```\nUse the same convention as the slides and state the asymptotic result.",
      hint: "Sum the inner-loop control cost 2i + 2 for i = 1..n, and remember that the body runs n(n+1)/2 times.",
      solution: "Initialization: 1.\nOuter loop control: 2n + 2.\nInner loop control for a fixed i: 1 + (i+1) + i = 2i + 2.\nSum over i = 1..n: 2·n(n+1)/2 + 2n = n² + 3n.\nBody: 2 units per execution, and the body runs n(n+1)/2 times: n² + n.\n\nT(n) = 1 + (2n + 2) + (n² + 3n) + (n² + n) = 2n² + 6n + 3.\n\nSo T(n) is Θ(n²). The triangular loop does about half as many body executions as the n×n loop, but the growth rate is the same."
    },
    {
      id: "ch1-ex-3",
      title: "Prove a quadratic bound",
      prompt: "Show that T(n) = 3n² + 5n + 2 is in O(n²) by giving explicit constants c and n₀. Then argue that it is also in Ω(n²) and therefore in Θ(n²).",
      hint: "For n ≥ 1, replace 5n and 2 with multiples of n². For the lower bound, drop the positive lower-order terms.",
      solution: "Upper bound: for n ≥ 1, 5n ≤ 5n² and 2 ≤ 2n², so\n3n² + 5n + 2 ≤ 3n² + 5n² + 2n² = 10n².\nTake c = 10 and n₀ = 1. Therefore T(n) ∈ O(n²).\n\nLower bound: for n ≥ 1, 3n² + 5n + 2 ≥ 3n². Take c = 3 and n₀ = 1, so T(n) ∈ Ω(n²).\n\nSince T(n) is in both O(n²) and Ω(n²), it is in Θ(n²)."
