      prompt: "A program repeatedly evaluates n! for 0 ≤ n ≤ 12. Compare recomputing each factorial with pre-computing a lookup table. What resource is saved, what resource is spent, and why does the slide stop at 12! for a 32-bit int?",
      hint: "12! = 479,001,600. Check the largest 32-bit signed integer, 2,147,483,647.",
      solution: "Recomputation uses O(n) multiplications per query and O(1) extra space. A lookup table stores 13 values (0! through 12!) and answers each query in O(1) time; the table costs Θ(1) space here because the bound is fixed, or Θ(k) if generalized to k pre-computed values.\n\nThe table saves time and spends space. It stops at 12! because 12! = 479,001,600 fits in a signed 32-bit int (maximum 2,147,483,647), while 13! = 6,227,020,800 does not. Storing 13! in a 32-bit int would overflow."
    }
