  summary: [
    "A program combines a data structure (how data is organized) with an algorithm (the steps that use it).",
    "An ADT is a logical contract of values and operations; a data structure is a physical implementation. One ADT can have many implementations.",
    "Data structures are primitive or non-primitive, and non-primitive structures are linear or non-linear.",
    "A valid algorithm is correct, concrete, deterministic, finite, terminating, efficient, general, and has specified inputs and outputs.",
    "Exact operation counts follow fixed conventions; asymptotic analysis then keeps only the growth rate.",
    "Big-Oh is an upper bound, Big-Omega a lower bound, and Big-Theta means both bounds meet.",
    "Best, worst, and average case describe input classes, not bounds; do not confuse “worst case” with “upper bound”.",
    "Space is analyzed with the same tools; the space/time trade-off asks which resource you are willing to spend."
